&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20141110022654/http:/typophile.com/wiki/Chuffed"&gt;Chuffed&lt;/a&gt; (&lt;em&gt;adj&lt;/em&gt;): Quite pleased.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In &lt;a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20141110022654/http:/typophile.com/wiki/Typophile"&gt;Typophile&lt;/a&gt; &lt;a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20141110022654/http:/typophile.com/wiki/Terminology"&gt;Terminology&lt;/a&gt;; it&amp;rsquo;s rather the opposite of &lt;a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20141110022654/http:/typophile.com/wiki/Squoosh"&gt;Squoosh&lt;/a&gt; as coined by &lt;a href="http://web.archive.org/web/20141110022654/http:/typophile.com/wiki/Miss%20Tiffany"&gt;Miss Tiffany&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As the orginal post also illustrates, less useful terminology has also been developed, based loosely around the lesser Chuff (&lt;em&gt;intransitive verb&lt;/em&gt;): to produce noisy exhaust or exhalations : proceed or operate with chuffs&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>